<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ça Ira</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">first heard in May <a href="/years/1790">1790</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Ça ira</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;Ça ira&quot; (French for &quot;It'll be fine&quot;) was first heard in May 1790, in the early years of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a>. Its words were written by Ladré, a former soldier who made his living as a street singer. The tune was a popular contredanse, &quot;Le carillon national&quot;, by Jean-Antoine Bécourt, a musician of the Théâtre Beaujolais; Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marie-antoinette">Marie Antoinette</a> is said to have often played it on her harpsichord.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1792 the warship La Couronne was renamed <b>Ça Ira</b> after the song.</blockquote><a href="/songs/ca-ira">Ça Ira</a></li></ul></section>
